    "product_id": "dc673019-6651-4782-98cb-45efb7fec036",
    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KPSR 071031\nAFDPSR\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Phoenix AZ\n331 AM MST Sat Nov 7 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n- Elevated flows will continue on the Salt and Gila rivers through \nat least early next week due to dam releases. \n\n- Dry weather with occasional breeziness and temperatures within a \nfew degrees of the daily normals will be common through much of next \nweek. \n\n&&\n\n.SHORT TERM /Today through Monday/... \nEarly morning objective analysis depicts the central circulation of \nlow pressure system cutting off from the northern stream and \ndescending into SW Arizona. A jet streak on the western periphery of \nthis circulation will ensure additional equatorward propagation over \nthe next 48 hours taking the cold core and synoptic ascent well into \nnorthern Mexico. In its wake, sharp high pressure ridging folded \ntowards the Great Basin/central Rockies will promote surface \npressure rises and subsident, dry air filtering into the forecast \narea. Periods of nocturnal/morning breezy E/NE winds will be common \nover portions of the area in this typical cold season pattern, \nhowever wind speeds should not be very strong or seasonally \nanomalous. Under the building ridge through the weekend, H5 heights \nwill rise into a 580-584dm range allowing modest warming versus the \npast several days. Numerical guidance spreads are rather narrow \nyielding excellent forecast confidence of high temperatures within a \nfew degrees of the daily normals while overnight lows warm several \ndegrees above normal. \n\n&&\n\n.LONG TERM /Tuesday through Friday/... \nHemispheric pattern amplification driven by an unusually early \nseason sudden stratospheric warming (SSW) event will be the main \nstory for the latter half of next week with large ensemble forecast \nspread persisting. For much of the week, northern stream jet energy \nwill settle over southern Canada while weak low amplitude waves in \nthe southern stream remain sequestered over northern Mexico. As a \nresult, subsident ridging with nearly steady midtropospheric heights \nwill define the pattern over the SW Conus yielding high confidence \nof a very tranquil, near persistence forecast carrying on from early \nin the week. By the end of the week, effects from disruption of the \npolar vortex will be manifested in poleward retrogressive ridging \nwhile deep negative height anomalies descending equatorward over the \nNE Conus and East Pacific. However, the specifics regarding depth, \npositioning, and evolution of features within the greater synoptic \npattern remain highly uncertain. Recent operational models peel a \nnotable shortwave from the east Pacific trough towards the SW \nConus/northern Mexico late in the week, however this type of \nsolution is not consistent among NAEFS and EPS members. Given the \namplification phase, wide ensemble spread and large run-to-run \nvariations are likely the next several days resulting in low \nforecast confidence for next weekend.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...Updated at 1030Z.\n\nSouth Central Arizona including KPHX, KIWA, KSDL, and KDVT: \nWinds will continue to stay out of the E/NE through the entire TAF\nperiod with speeds generally aob 10 kt through the period. Some\noccasional gusts in the upper teens are possible in the afternoon.\nSkies will be clear to mostly clear through the period.\n\nSoutheast California/Southwest Arizona including KIPL and KBLH: \nNo aviation impacts through the period with overall light winds \nfavoring the N/NW at both TAF sites through the period. Skies will\nremain clear.\n\n&&\n\n.FIRE WEATHER...\nDry conditions will prevail across the region through the majority \nof next week with some enhanced morning ridge top and terrain gap \nbreeziness during the beginning of the week. Minimum afternoon \nhumidity levels will primarily fall into a 20-30% range in western \ndistricts and 30-50% in eastern districts with a tendency for drying \ntowards the middle/end of the week. This will follow fair to good \novernight recovery in western districts and good to excellent levels \nin eastern districts. Outside of the early week breeziness, wind \nspeeds will be light with directional tendencies towards a \nnortherly or easterly component. \n\n&&\n\n.PSR WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...\nAZ...None.\nCA...None.\n&&\n\n$$\n\nSHORT TERM...18\nLONG TERM...18 \nAVIATION...Kuhlman\nFIRE WEATHER...18\n",
